  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1030 PM MST Thu Nov 12 2026\n\nThis evening's H500 hand analysis map has a highly amplified\nridge over the Rockies...extending through central Canada and \nacross Hudson Bay. The ridge is being flanked by a large digging\ndownstream trough over the East and a pair of upstream cyclones\nover the EPac approaching the coastline. The ridge will \ntranslate to the High Plains tomorrow and br further squeezed \nbetween the two troughs but it's influence and the southerly \nflow ahead of the upstream system will keep another mild day in \nplace. Highs will generally be around 5 to 10 degrees above \nnormal even with mid to high cloudiness drifting through.\n\n\nCOOLER, WETTER CHANGE HEADING INTO THE WEEKEND:\n\nWater vapor shows a lead system being kicked toward the Baja \nthis evening by a much stronger cyclone dropping out of the Gulf\nof AK. A weak AR associated with this southern system will allow\nthe first influx of moisture to be pulled across the 4 Corners\non southerly flow as early as tomorrow morning...though the \nbulk of it will be remain well to the South. This moisture will \nbe more of a primer for a stronger push of moisture moving in \ntomorrow night. A moderate AR is associated with the northern \ncyclone which will be impinging upon the Sierras through much of\ntomorrow as it drops southward. As it drops across the southern\nflank of the upstream terrain IVT suggests we get a more robust\npush of moisture into our eastern and southern CWA by late \ntomorrow evening. Don't expect much in accumulating \nprecipitation until this secondary moisture arrives though some \nhigh elevation orographics snow showers and sprinkles in the \nlower elevations could be possible by sunset. Luckily this \nsystem is not brining the highly anomalous moisture as the past \nfew and even though it drops in negatively tilted...will be \nshort lived. Though not overly cold much of the precipitation \nabove 8500-9000 feet should locked in as snow. The persistent \nmoist southerly flow will allow the southern San Juans to once \nagain catch the best precipitation for the event. Other ranges \nwill do better Saturday morning and early afternoon as the front\npasses through and the overall dynamics aloft are the \nstrongest. Snow levels hover around 9K before fropa then drop to\nmountain bases by sunset as the storm is exiting. Storm totals \nof 4-10 inches are most likely over the southern San Juans above\n9K with ensemble spot forecasts suggesting higher amounts near \na foot in a few favored locations. A bigger challenge is \nforecasting possible impacts as much of the snow should melt as \nit hits the roadways during the daylight hours...leaving only a \nfew hours of slushy/snowpacked roads near sunrise on Saturday \nand over the northern passes after sunset before the system \nexits. For now will continue to hold off on any winter \nhighlights with other mountain locations only seeing a few\ninches at best. The liquid precipitation forecast across the \nlower elevation basins of the San Juan River remain in the \nquarter to half inch range as the highest probability outcome \nand hydrographs show only minimal responses at this point. \n\nLOOKING AHEAD:\n\nA cooler and drier Sunday leads to a warmer Monday as ridging\nonce again translates by as a stronger trough digs back upstream.\nThe passage of this trough will brings another round of valley \nrain and mountain snow by mid week. IVT forecasts suggest a\nmore robust push of moisture and QPF amounts increase as a \nresult. Unsettled conditions persist into the end of the week as\nlow pressure looks to take over much of the West. Forecast \nconfidence is very low at this time due to differences in the \ntiming, strength, and track of this late week system.",
